sdio: add a direct /dev/artosyn_sdio chardev (option 2: reimplement the
SDIO userspace backend ourselves, instead of vendoring the closed binary)

Depends on 0010-sdio-fix-double-free-when-probe-fails.patch (this file's
probe()/remove() edits sit right next to that patch's own fail-path
fix and expect it already applied).

Background: this project's DRV-mode transport (ar8030-transport-tx over
/dev/ar_mdev0) plateaus around 5-7Mbps regardless of the AR8030's own
reported link bandwidth/MCS -- confirmed on real hardware by raising the
radio's channel bandwidth from 5MHz to 20MHz (see ar8030-transport's own
README, "Bandwidth: the real bottleneck") and finding the actual
achieved throughput unchanged. Meanwhile the *stock* vendor streamer
(ar_ldyhs_sky) reaches ~20-25Mbps on the same hardware. Loading the
vendor's own prebuilt artosyn_sdio.ko + daemon_sdiov12 (from an official
Ascent firmware release) side-by-side with this project's own
ar8030-transport-tx, unmodified, confirmed ~18Mbps -- i.e. the DRV-mode
kernel path itself, not anything in ar8030-transport, is the bottleneck.

daemon/dev8030/sdio8030/sdio_dev.c (this SDK's own daemon-side SDIO
backend, gated by DEV_8030_SDIO, already fully implemented -- just never
built) expects nothing more than a plain, directly-openable character
device at /dev/artosyn_sdio: open(O_RDWR), poll() for POLLIN, read() (up
to ~20KB), write() (block-aligned to 512 bytes). This driver's own
bus/sdio.c already implements everything needed to serve that -- probe/
firmware-download/IRQ handling, and even artosyn_sdio_write()/
artosyn_read() functions with exactly the right signature -- it just
never exposed them as a character device (only control/ar_chardev.c
does, built around the heavier ar_mdev<N>/oal_mdev.c message-multiplexing
architecture DRV mode uses, which is the likely source of the DRV-mode
throughput ceiling: oal_mdev.c's send() path allocates a fresh skb per
write and queues it through oal_send_msg_req(), built for multiplexing
up to 8 ar_mdev instances over one physical channel with its own retry/
backoff for a full queue -- overhead a single physical device doesn't
need). This patch adds that missing chardev, calling straight into the
existing artosyn_sdio_write()/artosyn_read(), bypassing oal_mdev.c's
multiplexing layer entirely.

UPDATE 1: root-caused and fixed a race found during actual SDIO daemon
bring-up (ar8030d built with USING_8030SDIO=ON, run with -i 1). The
kernel module's own RX workqueue (ar_sdio_rx_func(), servicing
oal_mdev.c's DRV-mode queue) is wired unconditionally to every SDIO
RX-ready interrupt/reg_check pass -- it calls artosyn_read() on its own
kernel thread regardless of which userspace interface is actually
selected at runtime (that's a daemon-side -i choice the kernel has no
visibility into). With the chardev registered, that workqueue thread and
a chardev reader (sdio_chardev_read() -> artosyn_read()) both call
artosyn_read() on the same struct artosyn_sdio_device concurrently,
racing on its single reading/read_offset/read_valid_size state --
confirmed on real hardware as a continuous "artosyn_read: busy" (-EBUSY)
/ "dev->read_valid_size == dev->read_offset" (-EAGAIN) flood in dmesg,
each caller starving the other, as soon as the daemon's SDIO event loop
started polling.

UPDATE 2: UPDATE 1's own first fix was itself wrong and broke plain DRV
mode entirely. It skipped the oal_queue_work() call whenever
sdio_chardev_registered was set -- but that flag just means the device
node *exists* (true unconditionally from probe() onward, regardless of
which -i mode the daemon was ever started with), not that some daemon
actually has it open. Confirmed on real hardware: this disabled
oal_mdev.c's only RX feed on every single boot, including plain DRV mode
where nothing ever touches /dev/artosyn_sdio at all -- total_rx_pkts
stuck at 0 forever, deterministically, every boot. Fixed for real with a
proper open-count (sdio_chardev_open_count, incremented/decremented in
sdio_chardev_open()/release()): the workqueue is now only skipped while
something has the chardev actually open. The read_offset/read_valid_size
bookkeeping right above each site stays unconditional either way, since
the chardev's own poll() (artosyn_sdio_read_condition()) depends on it.

UPDATE 3: with 0012-sdio-write-wait-for-real-interrupt-not-100ms-poll.patch
also applied (fixing artosyn_sdio_write()'s own wait), -i 1 bring-up got
further -- BB_GET_STATUS worked immediately -- but the daemon's data
socket then stalled permanently right after bb_socket_open(), stuck at
"socket try init" / sock_wait_usb_cmd (see daemon/sock_node_rpc.c /
sock_node.c) forever, with dmesg showing *no* write-wait-timeout at all
even minutes in (0012's own 2000ms timeout would have logged one by
then if a real write had even been attempted). Root cause:
sdio_chardev_poll() only ever called poll_wait() on dev->tx_q, but the
chip's so_open acknowledgment arrives as an RX-ready mailbox event, and
that branch's own wake_up_interruptible_all(&dev->rx_q) call was
commented out upstream (oal_mdev.c's DRV-mode consumer never waits on
rx_q, only via its own workqueue) -- so poll() was never woken by RX-only
activity. At this exact point in the handshake there is no TX traffic
yet either (the so_open command itself already went out), so nothing
was coincidentally re-waking poll() the way ordinary bidirectional
traffic might. Fixed: uncommented the rx_q wakeup in both
artosyn_sdio_irqhandler()'s and artosyn_sdio_reg_check()'s rx-ready
branches (harmless for DRV mode -- nothing there waits on rx_q either),
and sdio_chardev_poll() now calls poll_wait() on both tx_q and rx_q.

UPDATE 4: even with UPDATE 3 and 0012 both applied, a *reassurance* test
against the vendor's own real artosyn_sdio.ko + daemon_sdiov12 (Ghidra-
compared binaries, same technique as 0012) on an identically fresh boot
(nothing else having touched the chip first, to rule out cross-module-
reload contamination as a confound -- confirmed real: the vendor daemon
itself was unresponsive on a boot where our own module had already run
first, and perfect on a genuinely fresh one) showed the vendor's stack
holding a clean, stable ~14-18Mbps for minutes with zero stalls, while
our own -i 1 stack, tested the exact same way, still recurringly hit
"bb_socket_write made no progress" every few seconds, capping real
throughput around ~3Mbps. dmesg showed no write-wait-timeout during
these stalls either (ruling out 0012's own wait as the cause) -- this is
a different bug from UPDATE 3's, since the daemon's own log confirmed
data was flowing, just stalling repeatedly rather than never starting.

Comparing artosyn_sdio_irqhandler()/artosyn_sdio_reg_check() against the
vendor's real decompiled equivalent found a real, general design
difference (not a bug in the size-decode math, which 0004 already fixed
and which matches the vendor exactly): the vendor wakes a *single shared*
waitqueue for every mailbox event -- either channel, rx-ready, and
tx-ready alike -- rather than separate per-condition queues. A writer
blocked on tx_q specifically only gets a chance to re-check its own
condition when a genuine TX-ready event fires; the vendor's design gives
every blocked waiter a chance to re-check on *any* mailbox activity,
however unrelated, which is strictly more chances per second to notice a
state change than our previous split-queue design offered. Matched here
by having every wake site (mailbox_q's per-channel loop, rx-ready,
tx-ready, in both artosyn_sdio_irqhandler() and artosyn_sdio_reg_check())
wake both tx_q and rx_q, rather than introducing one literal shared
queue (a bigger structural change for the same practical effect). Not
yet re-tested on real hardware as of this patch.

Status: builds cleanly. UPDATE 1-3's fixes hold (chardev opens/registers,
no busy/EAGAIN race, DRV mode unaffected, socket-open handshake
succeeds). UPDATE 4's shared-wake change is the current attempt at the
still-open "recurring stall under sustained load, vendor stack doesn't
have this" problem -- not yet confirmed to fix it. See ar8030-transport's
own README for the full investigation writeup.

